Date: Tue, 21 Sep 93 23:43:07 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: GAME: Improving on Tit-For-Tat

This months Sci Am, referring to recent paper in Nature, describes a 
strategy that does better than Axelrod's tit-for-tat. 

What works better? A strategy that is usu. reciprocal like tit-for-tat, 
but does not expose itself to the ecological risk of establishing so 
many "nice" players that a wave of defectors can invade.

More interestingly, if a partner always cooperates, then Pavlov decides 
to defect, since it has nothing to lose.

