Subject:  Recovering From Objectivism

>From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
>Subject: Recovering Objectivist Seeks Source Material
>
>Re how I became aware of my anti-Objectivist tendencies:  basically,
>just recently it became crystal clear that I'm too emotional to be an
>Objectivist, and my emotions are often not connected with any
>intellectual/moral assessment of the thing at hand.  I think a lot of
>my internal conflict comes from the disagreement between what I feel
>and what I think I should be feeling.

Some psychology appears to be in order.  Ellis' book may be valuable to
you, but it was written as a polemic after he debated Nathaniel Branden
back when he (Branden) was still the Grand Inquisitor of the Eastern
Orthodox Church of Objectivism.  Didn't look very interesting to me.
Maybe more general stuff from his school of thought, rational-emotive
psychology, would be better.

Actually, the most interesting critique of Rand on psychological grounds
that I've ever encountered has been Branden's, from the period after he
recovered from being Grand Inquisitor of the E.O.C.O.  Can't think of
any specific works to suggest, though, because he has this annoying
habit of interspersing profound insights amongst much more pedestrian
material - which would be more interesting if he didn't have such a
tendency to repeat himself in book after book.

Relevant to the problem you list above, we often subconsciously and
inarticulately make judgements and decisions based upon information
that wouldn't be available to our conscious mind in such a way that we
can articulate it.  For example, your conscious mind may be quite busy
articulating information having to do with nothing but your job, while
your social life may be suffering, making you lonely.  Branden mentions
this in passing, but doesn't elaborate upon it enough for me.

I've also always found anything by Robert Anton Wilson to be good for
what ails me psychologically.

Beyond that, your "symptoms" don't really seem to indicate any sort of
pathology at all to me.  They seem like part of being alive as a
human being.

