Date: Wed, 22 Sep 93 08:38:16 CDT
From: eder@hsvaic.boeing.com (Dani Eder)
Subject: SPACE: limits on launch costs

In theory, the lower bound for launch cost to orbit is simply the
cost of raw energy to get there.  Orbital velocity is 7900 m/s,
so the energy required is 31 MJ/kg, or 8.7kWh.  At $0.08/kWh for
electricity, this is $0.70/kg.  This assumes 100% electric-to-kinetic
energy conversion efficiency.

In practice, efficiencies are less than 100%, and you have to include
amortization and maintenence of the launch system.  Therefore the
system that has the lowest cost/kg will tend to be one that delivers
the most kg of payload per kg of capital equipment.  Airports and
Rocket launch complexes cost about the same to build, but the former
is used as much as once per minute (for O'Hare) and the latter is
used around once per month (for Kennedy).  The factor of 40,000:1 in
utilization is a major reason you can afford to fly out of O'Hare but
not Kennedy.

Electromagnetic catapults have high efficiencies (75% or better), and
high throughputs (limited by waste heat dissipation and how big a 
power supply (power line)) you have), so I would rank them as very
high on the theoretical capabilities.  As far as practical use, the
highest speed for a multi-kg projectile is around 1 km/s, so the 
development has a ways to go.  Gas guns have been firing ~1kg projectiles
at 6 km/s for years, and the Livermore Laboratory gun in test now is
designed to fire 5kg at 4 km/s, and it cost $3 million to build.
In the near term, I expect gas guns to be much easier to get up and
running and reaching low hundreds of $/kg at EXISTING TRAFFIC RATES,
and then being surpassed by electromagnetic systems in the longer
run when higher traffic demands it.  100kg at one minute intervals
launches in a day what the Shuttle launches in a year, so you have
2-3 orders of magnitude the launch capacity.  Right now there wouldn't
be any customers who needed that much.

